    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Aqueduct wells in Edo",
    text: "Edo drew its water through two great aqueducts, the Kanda and, from 1654, the Tamagawa, which carried river water from forty kilometres west by open channel and then underground in wooden and stone pipes. In the city, the pipes fed square wooden wells, where tenement dwellers let down buckets on a rope or a pole. Each year in the seventh month the wells were cleaned by the whole block together, ido-sarai. Low-lying Honjo and Fukagawa, where the water was salty, relied on water sellers, who brought it by boat and sold it by the bucket.",
    wiki: ["Tamagawa Aqueduct", "Edo"],
