    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The ie: one heir, one house",
    text: "The Tokugawa household, ie, was a continuing corporation more than a family: its name, land, trade and ancestors passed intact to a single heir, usually the eldest son. Younger sons became adopted heirs elsewhere, apprentices, or labourers; daughters married out with a dowry. A household without a son adopted one, often by marrying a daughter to an adopted son-in-law, mukoyōshi, who took the family name. Branch households, bunke, could be set up with a small share of land and owed deference to the main house, honke. Thatched farmhouses were rebuilt with the help of the village roof-thatching association.",
    wiki: ["Ie (Japanese family system)", "Mukoyōshi", "Minka"],
