    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The family temple and the butsudan",
    text: "To root out Christianity, the Tokugawa shogunate required every household to register with a Buddhist temple, which certified each year that its members were not Christian. This danka system made the temple the family's undertaker. At death, the priest gave the dead a posthumous Buddhist name, kaimyō, written on a black lacquered tablet, ihai, placed in the household altar, butsudan, where the family offered rice, water, incense and prayers each morning. Memorial services came on the seventh and forty-ninth days, then on the first, third, seventh, thirteenth and later anniversaries, up to the thirty-third or fiftieth.",
    wiki: ["Danka system", "Butsudan", "Dharma name"],
