    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Retirement, inkyo",
    text: "In Edo Japan an old head of household retired, inkyo, handing the headship, the family name and the management of land or shop to his heir, often while still in his fifties. The retired couple might move to a smaller separate house or room on the property, living on a set share of the income. Care of the old fell to the heir and above all to his wife, the daughter-in-law, whose position improved only when she in turn became the senior woman of the house. Moral books praised filial children, and domains rewarded exemplary sons and daughters with public commendations and rice.",
