    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Terakoya and the merchant's apprentice",
    text: "By the late Edo period perhaps half of boys and a fifth of girls in towns attended terakoya, private schools run by priests, samurai, doctors or townspeople, learning to write letters and to use the abacus from copybooks such as the Teikin ōrai. At ten or twelve, a merchant's son or a farmer's younger son might go into service, hōkō, in a city shop as a detchi, living and eating there, sweeping and running errands for years for board and small pay. After a decade he could become a clerk, tedai, and with luck and his master's blessing open a branch shop of his own.",
