    title: "Porters and post stations on the Tōkaidō",
    text: "The five great highways of Edo Japan were walked, not driven: wheeled carts were mostly banned on them. At each of the fifty-three post stations of the Tōkaidō, the station had to keep 100 porters and 100 packhorses ready for official travellers, drawing on neighbouring villages, sukegō, when it ran short, which peasants bitterly resented. Kago bearers carried travellers in slung palanquins; hikyaku runners carried letters and money in relays, Edo to Osaka in as little as three days. Inns, teahouses and souvenir sellers lined the road, as Hiroshige's prints show.",
