    text: "Because taxes were paid in rice, the domains shipped their rice to Osaka, 'the kitchen of the realm', and sold it there through merchant warehouses for silver. At the Dōjima Rice Exchange, chartered in 1730, traders bought and sold rice tickets and dealt in what were effectively futures. Merchant houses such as the Kōnoike and Mitsui grew rich lending to daimyō and running dry-goods stores. Officially merchants ranked below farmers and artisans, but samurai increasingly owed them money. In towns, wholesale houses, tonya, organised into licensed associations, kabunakama, controlled trade in oil, cotton, sake and more.",
    wiki: ["Dōjima Rice Exchange", "Kabunakama", "Mitsui"],
