    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Arita porcelain and the Korean potters",
    text: "Japanese porcelain began with Korean potters brought to Kyushu during Hideyoshi's invasions of Korea in the 1590s. Around 1616, one of them, known as Yi Sam-pyeong, is said to have found porcelain stone at Izumiyama in Arita. The Nabeshima domain controlled the kilns closely, keeping techniques secret, and the Dutch East India Company shipped Arita wares, called Imari after the port, to Europe from the 1650s when Chinese exports faltered. Work was organised in climbing kilns shared by many workshops, with decorators in overglaze enamels, such as the Kakiemon family, forming a separate, guarded group.",
    wiki: ["Arita ware", "Yi Sam-pyeong", "Kakiemon"],
