    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Daiku: the Japanese carpenter",
    text: "Japanese buildings were timber-framed, and the carpenter, daiku, was the chief builder, drawing the plan and cutting the frame. Japanese saws and planes cut on the pull stroke, which let blades be thin and precise; joints were cut to lock without nails. A master kept a squared rule, sashigane, marked with proportional scales, and temple carpenters worked from secret rules of proportion, kiwari. Carpenters passed skills down in family lineages, with apprentices serving ten years. In Edo, where fire destroyed whole districts every few years, carpenters were among the best-paid workers, and the city's firemen were drawn from the building trades.",
