    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The Tenmei and Tenpō famines",
    text: "Cold summers and eruptions brought Edo Japan two great famines. In the Tenmei famine of 1782–87, made worse by the eruption of Mount Asama, the northern Tōhoku domains lost hundreds of thousands of people to hunger and disease; villages ate horses, straw and roots, and survivors abandoned their fields. In the Tenpō famine of the 1830s rice riots broke out in cities, and the former Osaka magistrate Ōshio Heihachirō led an uprising in 1837 for the starving. Afterwards domains built relief granaries and promoted sweet potatoes and other famine crops.",
