    text: "Every Edo village had a tutelary shrine to its ujigami, where villagers took turns in the annual festival duties, carried the god's portable shrine through the streets, and presented newborns. Buddhist temples handled death; shrines handled life, harvest and purity. People pooled savings in confraternities, kō, to send members on pilgrimage to Ise or to climb Mount Fuji. At times, waves of mass pilgrimage, okage mairi, swept millions to Ise, with servants and children leaving without permission. Wandering yamabushi and blind female mediums, itako, offered healing and messages from the dead.",
