    text: "Behind each Edo village lay its satoyama, the hills of coppiced oak, chestnut and pine it managed for fuel, charcoal, leaf litter and green manure. Villagers had rights to cut there on set days, under rules that limited what could be taken, and disputes between villages over these commons were fought in the courts. Townspeople cooked and warmed themselves at braziers fired with charcoal from specialist burners in the hills. After heavy logging in the seventeenth century, the shogunate and domains restricted cutting and encouraged replanting, and Japan's forests recovered over the eighteenth.",
