    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Rice, miso and pickles",
    text: "An Edo meal was ichijū issai, one soup, one side: rice, miso soup and pickles, with fish or vegetables if one could afford them. Peasants ate less rice than they grew, mixing it with barley, millet or daikon, and sold the rest for taxes. Households made their own miso from soybeans, salt and kōji mould, fermented in wooden tubs for a year or more, and pickled daikon in rice bran. In Edo, townspeople ate polished white rice, which caused beriberi, the 'Edo sickness', and bought noodles, tempura and sushi from street stalls. Soy sauce from Chōshi and Noda reached the city by river.",
