    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Factory girls in the silk filatures",
    text: "Raw silk was Meiji Japan's biggest export, and it was reeled by young women. The government's model filature at Tomioka opened in 1872 with French machinery, staffed at first by daughters of samurai. Hundreds of private filatures followed, especially in Suwa in Nagano, recruiting girls of twelve to twenty from poor farm families on contracts paid partly in advance to their parents. They worked thirteen to fifteen hours a day in steamy halls, lived in locked dormitories, and many fell ill with tuberculosis. Their story was told in Hosoi Wakizō's Jokō aishi of 1925 and later in Ah! Nomugi Pass.",
