    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The land tax and the tenant farmer",
    text: "The Meiji land tax reform of 1873 replaced the old rice levy with a money tax of 3 percent of assessed land value, paid by the owner whatever the harvest. In bad years or when rice prices fell, as in the Matsukata deflation of the 1880s, many smallholders lost their land to moneylenders and richer neighbours. By 1910 almost half of farmland was worked by tenants, who paid their rent in rice, often half the crop. Tenant disputes grew in the 1920s. The 1930s Depression brought hunger to Tōhoku villages, where some families sold daughters. American-directed land reform after 1946 finally broke the landlords.",
