    text: "Japan's industrialisation ran on coal from northern Kyushu and Hokkaido. At Miike, run by Mitsui from 1889, the state had earlier used convict labour. In the small Chikuhō pits, whole families went down together: husbands hewed and wives, often with babies, hauled the coal out in baskets and tubs, until women were barred from underground work in 1928. The painter Yamamoto Sakubei, himself a miner, recorded this life in hundreds of paintings now listed by UNESCO. Labour contractors ran company barracks. From 1939, Korean and Chinese workers were forced into the mines under brutal conditions.",
