    text: "From 1940 every Japanese household belonged to a neighbourhood association, tonarigumi, of ten or so families, through which the state distributed rations, ran air-raid drills, collected savings and scrap metal, and watched for dissent; a circulated board carried notices from house to house. Rice was rationed from 1941 and supplemented with barley, sweet potatoes and pumpkin. American air raids in 1945 burned most cities, and city children were evacuated to the countryside. After defeat, food was desperately short; people travelled to farms to trade kimono for rice, and bought from black markets around the railway stations.",
