    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Funerals, Obon and the war dead",
    text: "In modern Japan the Buddhist funeral and household altar continued, but cremation, once mostly for towns and some sects, became the norm, encouraged by public health rules. Each summer at Obon, families cleaned the graves, lit fires to guide the ancestors home, laid out food at the altar and danced the bon odori. The Meiji state added a new cult of the war dead, enshrined as kami at Yasukuni in Tokyo; by 1945 millions of Japanese households had a son or husband there. War widows were expected to raise their children, farm the land, and remain loyal to the husband's household.",
