    title: "Comb-pattern pottery and shell middens",
    text: "Neolithic Korea is called the Jeulmun, 'comb-pattern', period, after pots with incised herringbone lines. People lived in pit houses along rivers and coasts, fishing with nets, hooks and composite fishhooks, gathering shellfish, and hunting deer and boar. The shell midden at Dongsam-dong in Busan, used for thousands of years, holds oyster and mussel shells, bones of sea lions and whales, and obsidian from Kyushu, showing contact across the strait. The rock carvings at Bangudae in Ulsan, whose date is debated, depict whales being hunted from boats. Millet cultivation began on a small scale in the middle Jeulmun.",
