    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Paddies and longhouses of the Mumun",
    text: "Around 1500 BCE Korean farmers began making plain undecorated pottery, mumun, and farming seriously: rice in small bunded paddies, and millet, barley, wheat and beans in dry fields. At Okhyeon near Ulsan, excavated paddies are tiny terraced squares, and at Daepyeong on the Nam River, dry fields show ridges and furrows. Early Mumun families lived in long rectangular pit houses with several hearths, perhaps for extended families; later, at Songguk-ri, smaller round houses clustered in villages behind palisades. Semi-lunar stone reaping knives, like those of China, cut the rice ear by ear.",
