    text: "Goryeo potters made celadon, stoneware with a glaze coloured blue-green by a little iron fired in a reducing kiln; a Chinese visitor in 1123 called its colour the finest under heaven. In the twelfth century they invented sanggam, inlay: designs of cranes, clouds and willows were carved into the leather-hard clay, filled with white and black slip, and glazed over. The best kilns were at Gangjin and Buan in the south-west, working in long climbing kilns up hillsides, supplying the court and temples by sea. Mongol invasions in the thirteenth century began their decline.",
