    title: "Temples, the Tripitaka and cremation",
    text: "Buddhism was Goryeo's state religion. Kings sponsored great temples and the Palman Daejanggyeong, over 80,000 woodblocks of the Buddhist canon carved in 1237–48 as a plea for protection from the Mongols, now kept at Haeinsa. Temples owned land and slaves, ran inns and lent money, and many families sent a son to become a monk. Death rites were Buddhist: the body was often cremated, the bones placed in a jar or stupa, and a monk chanted through the forty-nine days. Great festivals, the Palgwanhoe and Yeondeunghoe lantern festival, mixed Buddhism with worship of mountain and dragon spirits.",
