    title: "Three years in hemp: Joseon mourning",
    text: "Joseon Korea followed Zhu Xi's Family Rituals more strictly than China did. When a parent died, the chief mourner wore coarse hemp, carried a bamboo or paulownia staff, and mourned for three years, in practice twenty-seven months, and some yangban men lived in a hut by the grave. Burial replaced cremation, in a mound sited by a geomancer on a hillside. Widows of yangban families were barred from remarrying: from 1485 the sons of a remarried widow were excluded from office. Filial sons and chaste widows were honoured with red memorial gates, hongsalmun.",
