    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Matchmakers, the wedding goose and the eldest son",
    text: "By the late Joseon, marriages were arranged between families, with a matchmaker and an exchange of the four pillars of each child's birth to check compatibility. The groom rode to the bride's house and presented a wooden wild goose, a symbol of lifelong fidelity, to her mother; the couple bowed to each other and shared a gourd of wine. The bride then moved to her husband's family, and was expected to serve her parents-in-law. From the seventeenth century inheritance shifted to favour the eldest son, who kept the rites and the main house; daughters got little and were written out of the lineage.",
