    title: "Transplanting, dure teams and the farmers' band",
    text: "In late Joseon, rice transplanting spread across southern Korea, raising yields and allowing barley as a winter crop, but needing lots of hands at once. Villages organised dure, cooperative labour teams of the village's men, which moved from field to field weeding and transplanting under a banner, with a farmers' band of gongs and drums, nongak, setting the pace. Smaller exchanges of labour between households were called pumasi. Most farmers were tenants on yangban land, paying half the crop in rent. Commoners also owed military cloth tax, which was notoriously abused.",
