    text: "By the eighteenth century, Korea had over a thousand periodic markets, jangsi, most meeting every five days in rotation, so that a village was within a day's walk of one every day. They were carried by pedlars, bobusang: the bobu carried goods such as cloth and small wares wrapped in cloth bundles, and the busang carried heavier goods like salt, pottery and fish on A-frame back carriers, jige. The pedlars had their own guilds with rules, officers and mutual aid, and the state used them as messengers and even militia. Seoul had licensed shops, the six licensed stores, sijeon.",
