    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Onggi jars, kimchi and gimjang",
    text: "Every Korean house had a platform, jangdokdae, lined with onggi, large brown earthenware jars glazed with ash and iron, which breathe through their walls and kept soy sauce, soybean paste, fish sauce, pickles and grain. Onggi were made by itinerant or village potters by coiling and paddling clay on a wheel. In late autumn women gathered for gimjang, salting and seasoning hundreds of cabbages and radishes, then burying jars of kimchi in the ground to keep through winter. Chilli pepper, from the Americas, entered kimchi only in the eighteenth century; before that it was salted and seasoned with ginger, garlic and fish sauce.",
