    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Ondol floors and the firewood hills",
    text: "The Korean house was heated by ondol: smoke and heat from the kitchen fire passed through flues under the stone floor of the main room before leaving by a chimney, so the family ate, sat and slept on a warm floor covered in oiled paper. By Joseon times ondol covered most rooms in most houses, which was comfortable in the Korean winter but burned great quantities of firewood, pine needles and brushwood. By the nineteenth century many hills near villages had been stripped bare. Gathering fuel was a daily task for boys and men, carried home on the A-frame jige.",
