    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The village seodang and the civil examination",
    text: "Korean boys learned to read in the seodang, a village school kept by a teacher, hunjang, in his house or a pavilion. They began with the Thousand Character Classic and the Children's Primer, Dongmong seonseup, and recited aloud until they could repeat each text; finishing a book was celebrated with rice cakes, chaekgeori. Yangban sons went on to study the classics for the civil examination, gwageo, which fixed their families' standing. Hangul, created by King Sejong in 1443, was easier to learn and was used by women and commoners for letters and novels, while men of standing wrote in classical Chinese.",
