    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Mudang and the gut",
    text: "Alongside Confucian rites led by men, Korean households, especially their women, turned to mudang, shamans, almost all women. In a gut, a ritual lasting from hours to days, the mudang danced in layered costumes to drums and cymbals, received the gods and the dead into her body, and spoke with their voices, curing illness, sending the dead peacefully on, or bringing luck to a household or a fishing fleet. Households also kept small shrines to the house gods: Seongju for the house, Jowang for the kitchen, Samsin for childbirth. The Joseon state scorned shamans, but taxed them.",
