    title: "Hwangap: the sixtieth birthday",
    text: "In Korea, reaching sixty, a full cycle of the sexagenary calendar, was an occasion for the family's greatest feast. At the hwangap, children and grandchildren bowed to the parent in order of seniority and offered wine, before a table stacked high with fruits, rice cakes and sweets. Living to sixty was not common, and it marked the parent's passage into honoured old age. Daily care of the old fell to the eldest son and his wife, who lived with his parents; a good daughter-in-law served them their meals first, warmed their room and asked after their health each morning and evening.",
