    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The women divers of Jeju",
    text: "On Jeju Island, women dived for abalone, conch, octopus, sea urchins and seaweed, holding their breath for a minute or more, many times a day, in all seasons. Men had dived for tribute abalone in early Joseon, but by the eighteenth century the work had passed to women, the haenyeo, and in many households they were the chief earners. They worked in groups from the shore, floating on gourds, taewak, with nets, and warmed themselves at stone-walled fire rings, bulteok, where they also made decisions about the fishery collectively. From the 1890s many went to work in Japan, Russia and mainland Korea.",
